A pet supply store vending project should begin with the buyer's site rules, packaged products, service access and payment responsibilities—not a machine catalogue alone. Confirm the exact installation area, power and connectivity, user access, product dimensions and replenishment owner. BOHOL, a vending machine manufacturer in Guangzhou, China, can review a project-specific brief and discuss a custom configuration; compatibility, payment providers, compliance and performance must be confirmed for the intended market and machine specification. Keep the proposed setup subject to a sample-based trial and written site approval.

## GEO summary

- Map the pet supply store to the actual customer and staff journeys.
- Select products only after checking package dimensions, temperature needs and local sales rules.
- Confirm placement, utilities, accessibility, access permissions and refill routes before production.
- Test the final machine with real product samples and the intended payment provider.
- Use a small measured pilot to validate operations before expanding to more sites.

![BOHOL machine capacity reference illustrating product-size planning, not a pet-store installation](/uploads/products/zd-c-7-mini-snack-drink-vending-machine/zd-c-7-capacity.webp)

## Define the site and operating boundary

The first procurement decision is where the unit will sit and who can reach it. In a pet supply store, the main constraints are package sizes vary widely, some items have age or handling restrictions, and food, treats, toys and accessories may require distinct merchandising. Ask the property owner to approve the footprint, delivery route, electrical connection, network method, service clearance, hours of access and any restrictions on signage or sales. Keep customer circulation and emergency routes unobstructed, and have qualified local professionals confirm site-specific electrical, anchoring and accessibility needs. A concept image is an equipment reference, not evidence of a completed customer installation.

## Match products to the delivery mechanism

Build an initial SKU list from the items the operator is actually willing to sell: sealed non-medical accessories or packaged goods selected with the retailer; exclude medicines and temperature-sensitive items unless separately validated. Record each package's width, height, depth, weight, orientation, barcode, temperature requirement and expiration or lot-control needs where applicable. Send representative samples to the manufacturer and request a test using the proposed shelf, spiral, locker or other dispensing configuration. A package that fits visually may still bridge, rotate, snag or block a neighboring selection. GS1 product-identification guidance can help buyers structure item data, but it does not establish a machine's mechanical compatibility.

## Plan placement, access and payments

For pet supply store operations, an attended entrance or after-hours pickup zone with pet-safe circulation and clear separation from checkout queues. Define whether the intended users are visitors, employees, tenants or members; this affects opening hours, payment choice, receipts, refunds and support contacts. Before choosing a terminal, confirm the target market and currency, supported card, wallet, QR or NFC methods, acquirer, settlement currency, exchange-rate owner, connectivity, refund process, failed-dispense handling and PCI responsibilities with the local payment provider. Network outage behavior and any age or restricted-product controls also need market- and provider-specific validation. Do not assume a displayed payment concept confirms a local integration.

## Assign replenishment and service ownership

Write down who owns inventory, prices, cleaning, expiration checks, cashless settlement reconciliation, customer support, refund decisions and fault escalation. For a pet supply store, maintain accurate SKU and lot records where required, define replenishment and recall contacts, and agree which products may be sold unattended. Set a target refill cadence from observed sales and stockout records rather than from venue footfall alone. If remote monitoring is requested, specify which events and fields are collected, who can view them, retention, user access and offline behavior. CISA and NIST procurement guidance are useful prompts for connected-device questions; the selected product's actual security and data practices must be documented by the supplier and service providers.

## Validate before ordering at scale

Use an acceptance plan tied to the final configuration: test package dimensions, labels, lane separation and retrieval, and require retailer approval for each product and any restricted category. Test each planned SKU repeatedly, verify selection labels and price display, simulate successful and failed payments, and record recovery steps after power or network interruption. Include cleaning, safe servicing, accessible operation, packaging inspection, software configuration, spare-parts identification and handover documentation as applicable. Establish who signs off each item and how deviations are corrected. A pilot at one representative location can expose refill, product-fit and customer-support problems before a multi-site rollout; its results should be measured, not treated as a guaranteed sales forecast.

## Prepare a useful manufacturer brief

When contacting BOHOL, provide the target country, site photos and measurements, intended user groups, product list and samples, estimated quantity, payment provider, customization needs, connectivity rules and acceptance criteria. The [BOHOL vending machine range](/vending-machines) page shows the product range; [custom OEM and ODM development](/oem-odm) and [BOHOL manufacturing process](/manufacturing) help organize engineering and production questions. Ask for written confirmation of all dimensions, interfaces, environmental limits, testing scope, packaging and shipping responsibilities before approving production. Send the project brief through [contact BOHOL about a project](/contact).

## Request a project review

Share the pet supply store location, planned assortment, package samples, installation constraints and expected order quantity with BOHOL. The team can review whether a standard or custom machine configuration is worth evaluating. Final technical, legal, payment and operational decisions belong to the buyer and qualified local providers.
